    `Before there was a world, there was community. The Trinity — Father, Son, and Holy Spirit — existed in perfect, eternal, self-giving relationship before a single atom was spoken into being. When God said in Genesis 1:26, let us make mankind in our image, the us was not a grammatical quirk. It was a window into the nature of the God who was doing the making. You were made by a community, in the image of a community, for community. The isolation that modern culture presents as freedom — the curated, self-sufficient, digitally mediated existence that needs no one and is accountable to no one — is not freedom. It is a slow amputation of something essential to what you are. The early church in Acts 2 is the most vivid portrait in Scripture of what Christian community looks like when it is functioning as designed. They devoted themselves — the Greek word is proskartereo, which means to persist in, to attend constantly to, to hold fast — to the apostles' teaching, to fellowship, to the breaking of bread, to prayer. Four things, in constant, persistent, communal practice. And the result was not merely a pleasant social environment. Everyone was filled with awe. Signs and wonders were performed. People were added daily. The community was not the nice extra on top of the real Christian life. The community was the environment in which the real Christian life became possible. Hebrews 10:24–25 adds the most urgent dimension: not giving up meeting together, as some are in the habit of doing — and all the more as you see the Day approaching. The writer is connecting two things that might seem unrelated: the habit of gathering together and the imminent return of Christ. The approaching Day is not an argument for withdrawal and private spiritual preparation. It is an argument for more community, not less. The person who is genuinely watching for the return of the King lives more deeply invested in the body of Christ, not less — because the Day is coming when the body will be complete, and everything done in community now is preparation for the eternal community that is coming then.`
