    `The Great Commission is the most misunderstood mandate in the New Testament. It has been professionalised — turned into the special calling of missionaries, evangelists, and full-time ministers — in a way that has quietly absolved the ordinary believer of the responsibility it actually places on them. Jesus did not give this command to a select group of spiritually qualified professionals. He gave it to eleven men standing on a hillside in Galilee — men who had recently been hiding behind locked doors, who had doubted in His presence, who had no institutional infrastructure, no financial support, no strategy. He gave it to them, and through them, to every person who has ever received what they received. The grammatical structure of Matthew 28:19–20 is important: the main verb is not go. It is make disciples. Going is a participle — as you go, as you move through your ordinary life — make disciples. The command is not primarily about geographical movement. It is about intentional, ongoing, relational investment in the people God has already placed in your path. You do not need to cross an ocean to obey the Great Commission. You need to cross the room. The Samaritan woman at the well is the New Testament's most striking portrait of spontaneous, ordinary, untrained witness. She had one conversation with Jesus. She did not receive theological training before she went. She did not wait until she had all her questions answered. She left her water jar — the everyday, practical, completely ordinary object that represented everything she had come to do — and she went back to her town and said: come, see a man who told me everything I ever did. Could this be the Messiah? She shared what she knew from what she had encountered. The whole town came. And Jesus stayed two more days because of the harvest that her witness produced. She did not preach a sermon. She told a story. And it was enough.`
